<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Avon, Hampshire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hampshire Avon, Salisbury Avon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Avon is a river in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> that rises in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a>, flows south through Salisbury and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a>, and reaches the sea at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christchurch">Christchurch</a> Harbour. Its valley was an early route inland from the coast, and the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charford">Cerdicesford</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> links with the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>, is usually placed at Charford on its banks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-avon-hampshire.jpg" alt="River Avon, Hampshire" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bob Ford · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:River_Avon_at_Salisbury_-_geograph.org.uk_-_31013.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Avon begins as two separate streams. The western arm rises east of Devizes and drains the Vale of Pewsey, while the eastern arm rises at Easton Royal, east of Pewsey. They merge at Upavon and flow south across Salisbury Plain through Durrington, Amesbury and Salisbury, where the Nadder, Wylye, Bourne and Ebble converge. South of Salisbury the river flows along the western edge of the New Forest through Fordingbridge and Ringwood to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christchurch">Christchurch</a>, where it meets the River Stour and enters Christchurch Harbour. About half of the river is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a>; the rest is split between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a> and, since 1974, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>. Its name is a tautology, since Avon is the Proto-Brythonic word for river.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Many prehistoric sites lie on either side of the valley, the largest being the World Heritage Site zone of Stonehenge, Avebury and Associated Sites, followed by the fortified knoll of Old Sarum. Long-farmed pastures and arable fields line much of the valley, and the wealth they brought to landowners is shown by ten large listed houses with protected parks, among them Wilton House, Longford Castle and Breamore House.</p><p>A navigation from Salisbury to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christchurch">Christchurch</a> was authorised by an Act of 1664. Work began in 1675 and the route was completed in 1684, but it fell out of use around 1715. Canalised channels were dug to straighten sections of the river, and a lock near Longford Castle survives, rebuilt in brick as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pound-lock">pound lock</a> after flooding damaged the original in the early 18th century. In 1699 the freeholders of Ibsley and Fordingbridge petitioned the House of Commons that they could not comply with the Act, and court cases to enforce a right of navigation in 1737 and 1772 were indecisive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Avon is thought to contain more species of fish than any other river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. In 1993 the valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a> between Bickton and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christchurch">Christchurch</a> was designated a Site of Special Scientific Interest, and in 1996 almost the whole river, with its tributaries, was designated the River Avon System SSSI. A four-year project called STREAM began in September 2005 to benefit species such as Atlantic salmon and water-crowfoot, followed by a project called Living River from 2006 to 2010. The Pewsey Avon Trail and the Avon Valley Path follow much of its length.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “River Avon, Hampshire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, compiled in Wessex in the late ninth century, the kingdom was founded by Cerdic and his son Cynric, who landed on the south coast with five ships in 495 and in 519 ‘took the kingdom’ after a battle at Cerdicesford, probably Charford on the <b>Avon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
